thread:
eli: Seeing a weird spike in partial-json errors from our Java SSE client when traffic surges. Happens after ~30s of sustained burst, parser throws EOF mid-object. Anyone seen this pattern?

mira: Which parser are you using? Jackson? okhttp eventsource?

eli: Jackson streaming (JsonFactory.createParser) wired to an okhttp streaming body reader. Error looks like: `com.fasterxml.jackson.core.JsonParseException: EOF while parsing object` in the middle of the stream. Not a full reconnect — connection stays open.

devon: Could be backpressure: reader thread can't keep up, bytes arrive in very small chunks that split tokens. Jackson should handle split tokens if fed continuous bytes, but if we hand it fragmented buffers it's easy to mis-handle. Are you feeding by-lines or raw bytes?

eli: We're feeding raw byte[] from okhttp's source.read(buf). We accumulate into a ByteArrayOutputStream and pass chunks to parser when we see a newline. Seems fine for normal traffic. Under burst the newline sometimes comes after many small chunks and parser gets a partial object at the boundary.

alice: Can you paste the read loop? quick check — maybe you're calling parser.close() on intermediate errors or reusing the same parser instance incorrectly.

eli: Sure: ```java
byte[] buf = new byte[8192];
int r;
while ((r = source.read(buf)) != -1) {
  baos.write(buf, 0, r);
  if (baosContainsNewline(baos)) {
    byte[] frame = extractFrame(baos);
    JsonParser p = factory.createParser(frame);
    handle(p);
    p.close();
  }
}
```

mira: aha — extracting frames by newline and giving each frame to a new parser can break when SSE sends multi-line data: `data:` lines can be split and JSON may be sent across several `data:` events. Also if you cut at arbitrary bytes you can create partial UTF-8 sequences.

sam: Two quick mitigations: 1) parse incrementally with a single JsonParser over a continuous InputStream rather than creating/closing per-frame; 2) add an assembly buffer that only hands whole JSON objects to Jackson (e.g., depth counter for braces) so you don't attempt parse until complete.

eli: Good call. We were relying on newline detection only. If we switch to incremental parser, is there a risk of unbounded buffering?

devon: You can stream into Jackson's non-blocking parser (JsonFactory.createNonBlockingByteArrayParser) or use JsonParser with InputStream and let it block — but for async you probably want non-blocking + feeding chunks. That avoids reallocation and handles split tokens. Another option: implement a simple brace-matching state machine to know when an object is complete before giving bytes to Jackson.

alice: Also check for backpressure in your consumer: if handle(p) does expensive work on the IO thread, move it to a worker thread and apply a bounded queue. If the IO thread stalls, OS will deliver smaller TCP segments which makes fragmentation worse.

eli: Makes sense. A follow-up: we observe the connection doesn't drop, just parser errors. Would adding a heartbeat/keepalive help detect stalls earlier?

sam: Heartbeats help only for detecting silent disconnects. For this, you'd benefit from flow-control: pause reading when queued work > threshold, resume when drained. Okhttp allows async callbacks where you can control consumption.

ci-bot: Reminder: file a small infra incident if this impacts prod and tag eng-infra for network trace assistance.

eli: Thanks — plan: replace per-frame parser with a non-blocking JsonParser feed, add a small assembly buffer that checks brace depth, and move heavy processing off the read thread. Will report back after a canary deploy.

mira: If you want, I can prototype a tiny non-blocking feed example in the morning.

eli: Please. That'd help. :thumbsup:

sam: One more: watch for multi-byte UTF-8 boundaries when slicing arrays. If you slice mid-codepoint and hand to parser, you'll get decoding errors. Use a CharsetDecoder or only slice at codepoint boundaries if assembling manually.

eli: Great notes — I'll audit our slicing logic as well. Thanks everyone!
